  "narrative": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">CORDA Dataset shows early but tangible use: the record reports 1,366 downloads and one documented downstream reuse. It is an openly available multi-site COVID-19 imaging dataset, which supports visibility and reuse across clinical and computer science research.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">CORDA Dataset is a 2023 openly accessible scholarly dataset hosted on Zenodo. It assembles 3,852 COVID-19 imaging records across chest X-ray and CT modalities from four hospitals in Italy, with contributions from a multi-institution team spanning clinical and academic settings. The dataset\u2019s structure and availability make it a reusable resource for imaging and machine-learning work in the biomedical domain.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The clearest impact signal in the record is usage: Datacite reports 1,619 views and 1,366 downloads, indicating sustained interest in the dataset. That level of access is consistent with active discovery and download by researchers or practitioners seeking COVID-19 imaging data.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Reuse evidence is modest but concrete. The record includes one documented downstream reuse, and Datacite links the dataset to at least one cited scholarly work, showing that CORDA has already entered the research literature as a referenced data resource. Because the citation base is still small, the evidence points to emerging uptake rather than mature impact.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The publication record itself is currently quiet: OpenAlex lists zero citations, and there are no scite or event-data mentions captured here. In that context, the strongest interpretation is that CORDA Dataset is being accessed and reused, but its broader scholarly footprint is still developing.</p>",
